<DocPage
  title="Sipcode privacy policy"
  description="What the Sipcode CLI and its Codex plugin read on your computer, what they write there, and what they never do: no network calls, no telemetry, nothing uploaded."
  path="privacy/"
  heading="Privacy policy"
  updated="2026-10-16"
>
  <p class="note">
    Short version: Sipcode is a command-line tool that reads files on your computer and writes files on your computer.
    It makes no network calls, has no telemetry, no account and no sign-in, and uploads nothing.
    The Sipcode plugin for Codex runs the same local <code>sipcode</code> command and adds nothing to that.
    This page covers the Sipcode CLI (npm package <code>sipcode</code>), its MCP server and its Codex plugin.
    The source version of this policy is <a href="https://github.com/Anuj7411/sipcode/blob/main/PRIVACY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PRIVACY.md</a> in the repository.
  </p>

  <h2>Personal data we collect</h2>
  <p>
    None. Sipcode never sends anything to the developer or to anyone else, so nothing about you, your code or your
    prompts reaches us. Everything below happens only on your own computer.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>No analytics, no event pings, no error reporting service.</li>
    <li>No telemetry of any kind, not even an opt-out kind.</li>
    <li>No signup, no account, no signed-in mode, no API key prompt.</li>
    <li>No auto-update check, no version ping, no usage counter.</li>
    <li>No background daemon. Sipcode runs only when you run a command and exits when it is done.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Sipcode reads on your computer</h2>
  <p>Local files only, read-only, and only when you run a command (or ask the Codex plugin a question, which runs one command):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>~/.claude/projects/&lt;hash&gt;/*.jsonl</code>: the session transcripts Claude Code already writes on your computer. Used by <code>why</code>, <code>stats</code>, <code>receipt</code>, <code>benchmark</code> and <code>regress</code>.</li>
    <li><code>~/.codex/sessions/</code> and <code>~/.codex/archived_sessions/</code> (or the folder <code>CODEX_HOME</code> points to): the session logs OpenAI Codex writes on your computer. Used by <code>stats</code>, <code>today</code>, <code>forecast</code>, <code>trend</code>, <code>impact</code>, <code>why</code>, <code>receipt</code>, <code>drift</code>, <code>regress</code> and <code>limits</code>, and by the MCP session tools and <code>get_codex_limits</code>, when Codex is installed. <code>limits</code> and <code>today</code> also read the usage-limit readings Codex writes into those logs (percent used, window length, reset time, plan type).</li>
    <li>Your repository's source files, for <code>manifest</code>, <code>score</code> and <code>init</code>. Parsing happens in-process.</li>
    <li>Git history, through the <code>git</code> program already on your computer, for hot files and change frequency.</li>
    <li>The pricing tables shipped inside the npm package (Anthropic and OpenAI). They come with each release and are never fetched.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Sipcode writes on your computer</h2>
  <p>Every write is caused by a command you ran. There are no background writes.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>.sipcode/</code> files in your repository: manifest, receipts (HTML and PNG), stats HTML, badge JSON, score HTML, benchmark HTML, and <code>install-state.json</code> (when you last ran <code>rules --install</code> or <code>hygiene --install</code>, and the rules mode, used by <code>impact</code>).</li>
    <li>One marked block in your <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. <code>sipcode rules --uninstall</code> removes it exactly.</li>
    <li>Entries in <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> and hook scripts at <code>~/.claude/hooks/sipcode-*.mjs</code>, only when you run <code>sipcode hygiene --install</code>. <code>--uninstall</code> removes them. These scripts make no network calls either.</li>
    <li>The proxy hook, <code>~/.claude/hooks/sipcode-proxy.mjs</code> plus its entry in <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>, only when you run <code>sipcode proxy --install</code> or <code>sipcode init</code> (skip it with <code>init --no-proxy</code>). <code>sipcode proxy --uninstall</code> removes it. It runs inside Claude Code before each tool call, reads and writes only local files, and makes no network calls.</li>
    <li>The receipt PNG is copied to your clipboard, only when you run <code>sipcode receipt</code>, using your system's own clipboard tool.</li>
    <li>Local caches under <code>~/.sipcode/</code>, so repeat runs do not re-read every log:
      <ul>
        <li><code>~/.sipcode/usage-cache/claude-code.json</code> and <code>~/.sipcode/usage-cache/codex.json</code> (<code>today</code>, <code>forecast</code>, <code>limits</code>, <code>regress</code>): for each session log, its size and modified time, plus token counts, model names, timestamps, request ids, each Claude Code request's version and entrypoint and whether it followed a tool result, the session's working folder and the paths of files the agent read. Never message text, prompts, file contents or command output.</li>
        <li><code>~/.sipcode/drift/sessions-v3.jsonl</code> (<code>drift</code>): per-session metrics (session id, end time, token counts, cache-hit rate, duplicate-read tokens, and the project folder as an encoded name such as <code>C--Projects-app</code>). <code>~/.sipcode/drift/configs.jsonl</code> keeps the names of your Claude Code MCP servers over time. An older <code>~/.sipcode/drift/sessions.jsonl</code> from before v1.7.0 is no longer used and can be deleted.</li>
        <li><code>~/.sipcode/proxy-reads/&lt;session-id&gt;.jsonl</code> (the proxy hook's re-read check, Claude Code only): for each file Claude read in that session, its path, a sha256 hash of its contents, modified time, size, a rough token estimate, and the turn and time it was first read. Never the file contents. A <code>&lt;session-id&gt;.warmed</code> marker beside it records that the cache was filled once from the session transcript.</li>
        <li><code>~/.sipcode/proxy-stats/proxy-stats-*.jsonl</code> (the proxy hook, read by <code>sipcode proxy --stats</code>): one line per rewritten or deduplicated tool call, with its time, the tool name, which rewriter acted, the estimated tokens saved and an integrity score. Never the command output.</li>
        <li><code>~/.sipcode/proxy-signals/&lt;session-id&gt;.jsonl</code> (the proxy hook, Claude Code only): the search patterns Claude sent to Grep, Glob or a grep command in that session, with the tool name and the time, so a later read can return only the matching symbols.</li>
        <li><code>~/.sipcode/install-pending.json</code>: written only when <code>sipcode init</code> runs while a Claude Code session is active. It holds the hook script and settings paths so the next quiet <code>sipcode</code> command can finish the proxy install, and is removed once applied.</li>
        <li><code>~/.sipcode/benchmark-live/results.jsonl</code>: written only when you run <code>sipcode benchmark --vs-rtk --live</code>. One line per run with the task id, condition, model, token counts, the cost Claude Code reported and the duration. That command runs <code>claude --print</code>, which uses your Claude account and the network the way Claude Code always does.</li>
        <li><code>~/.sipcode/.star-nudge</code>: a marker file (its only content is <code>sipcode-star-nudge/1</code>) so the one-time "star on GitHub" line after <code>proxy --stats</code> is shown only once per machine.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it reads this</h2>
  <p>
    Only to answer the question you asked: token use and cost at API prices, usage limits, why a session was expensive,
    whether a tool got worse after an update, and the other reports listed in the
    <a href="https://github.com/Anuj7411/sipcode#readme" target="_blank" rel="noopener">README</a>. The caches exist only to make the next run faster.
  </p>

  <h2>Who receives it</h2>
  <p>
    Nobody. Sipcode does not upload or share anything. What it prints goes to your terminal. When you use the Codex plugin
    or the MCP server, Sipcode's printed answer goes to the agent you asked (Codex or Claude Code), on your computer, and from
    there it is handled under that agent's and its provider's own terms and privacy policy, like anything else you share in that chat.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
  <p>
    The caches stay on your disk until you delete them. They are never uploaded. To remove them, delete the folders, for example
    <code>rm -rf ~/.sipcode/usage-cache ~/.sipcode/drift</code>, or all of <code>~/.sipcode</code>. They are rebuilt on the next run; the only cost is a slower first run.
  </p>

  <h2>Your controls</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sipcode does nothing unless you run a command. There is no daemon, no installed service and no startup item.</li>
    <li>Delete <code>~/.sipcode/</code> at any time.</li>
    <li>Undo the optional changes with <code>sipcode rules --uninstall</code>, <code>sipcode hygiene --uninstall</code> and <code>sipcode proxy --uninstall</code>.</li>
    <li>Remove Sipcode with <code>npm uninstall -g sipcode</code>, and remove the Codex plugin from Codex's plugin list.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the no-network promise is checked</h2>
  <p>
    It is a property of the code, checked by a test. <code>tests/privacy/no-network.test.ts</code> scans every TypeScript file
    under <code>src/</code> and fails the build if it finds a runtime import of <code>node:http</code>, <code>node:https</code>,
    <code>node:net</code>, <code>node:dgram</code>, <code>node:tls</code> or <code>node:dns</code>, or a top-level
    <code>fetch(</code>, <code>new XMLHttpRequest</code> or <code>new WebSocket(</code>, including in the generated hook scripts.
    If that test fails on a release, that release breaks this policy: please
    <a href="https://github.com/Anuj7411/sipcode/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Honest scope</h2>
  <p>
    The guarantee covers Sipcode's own code. It does not extend to third-party libraries, to Claude Code or Codex themselves,
    or to your shell, operating system or editor. The source and the test are public so you can check them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two commands start another program that uses the network, and only when you run them: <code>sipcode update --run</code>
    runs <code>npm i -g sipcode@latest</code> (npm downloads the new version), and <code>sipcode benchmark --vs-rtk --live</code>
    runs <code>claude --print</code>. Without <code>--run</code>, <code>sipcode update</code> only prints the npm command.
    The Codex plugin never runs either of them.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This website (anuj7411.github.io/sipcode) is separate from the CLI and the plugin. It is hosted on GitHub Pages and counts
    page views with <a href="https://umami.is" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Umami Cloud</a>, which does not set cookies and
    records page-level data such as the page address, referring site, browser, operating system, screen size and language.
    The page loads fonts from Google Fonts and the Product Hunt badge image from Product Hunt, so those services and GitHub
    receive your browser's request (including your IP address) under their own privacy policies. Nothing the CLI or the plugin
    does is sent to this website.
  </p>

  <h2>Future changes</h2>
  <p>
    If a cloud feature ever ships, it will be opt-in, off by default, and announced in the version that introduces it.
    Changes to this policy are made in <a href="https://github.com/Anuj7411/sipcode/blob/main/PRIVACY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PRIVACY.md</a>
    and on this page, with a new date at the top.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about privacy: <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/support/`}>support</a>, or open an issue at
    <a href="https://github.com/Anuj7411/sipcode/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/Anuj7411/sipcode/issues</a>.
    Sipcode is built by Anuj Ojha.
  </p>
</DocPage>
